WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.233
"How to Preserve Your Sense of Smell"

00:00:07.302 --> 00:00:09.430
Based on a study
of nearly 2,000 people,

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.360
our sense of smell
declines as we age,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.400
similar to our loss in vision
and hearing.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.920
About a quarter of Americans
over the age of 50

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.320
suffer from
olfactory dysfunction,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.120
a difficulty identifying odors,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.460
which climbs to more than half
of individuals

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.140
between the ages
of 65 to 80,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.880
and about 80 percent
in those over the age of 80.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.860
And that was before COVID-19,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.940
which affected the smell

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.680
of nearly 50 percent
of sufferers.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.600
Typically, it was temporary,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.980
but as many as a 15 percent

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.020
of non-hospitalized
COVID survivors

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.420
were still experiencing problems
with the ability to smell

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.440
three or more months later,

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.380
and there are cases of it
lasting for years.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.520
As anyone with even
a simple head cold can tell you,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.960
when you lose
your sense of smell,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.240
you lose much of your sense
of taste as well.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.800
Between 75 to 95 percent
of what we think of as taste,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.720
may actually be smell.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.840
This was all
too vividly illustrated

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.480
by the case
of Algerian War soldiers

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.860
whose tongues
were cut out,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.380
remarkably reporting
little loss

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.000
of food and drink
flavor sensation.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.160
But hey, if loss of smell
leads to loss of taste,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.440
think of all the weight loss!

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.180
That was apparently the thought

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.320
behind the development
of a "novel nasal device,"

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.000
gag-inducing silicone tubes
you stick into your nostrils

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.160
to cordon off
your smell receptors.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.160
Researchers recorded
a drop in preference

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.740
for sugary foods and beverages
along with weight loss,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.620
but only among
the younger adults,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.200
presumably because the sense
of smell of the older adults

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.580
was already impaired.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.220
Making everything taste bland

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.960
may help you skip
a few doughnuts,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.240
but the flipside
is that people

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.480
with smelling difficulties
tend to add more salt.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.760
Older individuals
were found to require

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.880
two to three times more salt
than those who were younger

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.740
to achieve the same salty taste.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.100
It's no wonder
that the lifetime risk

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.300
of developing
high blood pressure

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.760
may exceed 90 percent.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.560
What can we do to preserve
our sense of smell?

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.900
Ambient air pollution

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.060
is associated with
olfactory dysfunction,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.280
thought to explain
why nasal biopsies

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.220
from residents of Mexico City,
living and dead,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.280
showed more lesions
and inflammatory changes

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.680
than those living
in low-pollution cities.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.100
We may not have a choice
where we live,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.280
but there is definitely
a direct source of air pollution

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.800
tied to loss of smell
we can all choose to avoid:

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:39.600
cigarette smoke.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.500
Smell loss can have
serious consequences,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.040
for example,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.640
missing a gas leak, smoke,
or spoiled food.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.080
If you have lost your sense
of smell and use natural gas,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.080
please consider buying
a gas detector.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.840
But, in terms of
direct disability,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.280
most people who are affected

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.340
don't even to seem to be aware
their smell is impaired,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.660
even when asked directly.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.960
Nearly 8 out of 10 elderly
individuals with smell loss

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.600
thought they had
normal smell sensitivity.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.540
Hearing loss, however,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.420
is considered a major cause
of global disability,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.060
ranking among
the top chronic conditions

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.800
affecting older adults.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.180
For far too long, though,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.700
as a National Academy
of Medicine report put it,

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.840
hearing loss has been

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.180
"relegated to the sidelines
of health care."

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.060
For those interested,
check out my video series

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.760
that starts with Age-Related
Hearing Loss Is Preventable,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.620
then a supplement
shown to help,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.320
and ending with a video
on dietary changes

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.340
that can help, too.

